.TH TAR 1
.SH NAME
tar \- tape archiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tar
[ key ] [ name ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tar
saves and restores directories and files on floppy diskettes, magtape or
other media.
Each directory name given is dumped with all files and
(recursively ) subdirectories in it.
.PP
.BR tar 's
actions are controlled by the
.I key
argument.
The
.I key
is a string of
characters containing at most one function letter and possibly one or
more function modifiers.
Other arguments to the command are file or
directory names specifying which files are to be saved or restored.
.PP
.B tar
will prompt the user to change the volume (disk or tape) when it is
filled up, allowing for the saved files to be spread across multiple
volumes.
The
.B s
option can be used to specify the number of blocks on
the volume.
.B tar
will not prompt the user to change media when extracting
files from a previous back-up, so the user must either run the
.B tar
command once for each volume, or select the volume which contains the
needed file.
.PP
The default output device is floppy drive 0
.RB ( /dev/f0 )
on PRO/VENIX,
RAINBOW/VENIX, and VENIX/11 on Micro PDP-11 systems; it is
magtape drive 0
.RB ( /dev/mt0 )
on other VENIX/11 systems.
This default
may be overridden with the
.B f
modifier (see below).
.PP
The function portion of the key is specified by one of the following
letters:
.TP 8
.B c
Create (initialize) a new diskette;
.B c
clears the diskette of all files,
and writes at the beginning of the diskette.
This command
implies
.BR r .
.TP
.B r
The named files are written on the end of the diskette or tape.
The
.B c
function implies this, and the medium must have been
previously initialized with a
.BR c .
If the output is longer than one
volume, subsequent volumes are written on from the beginning;
that is, they are automatically initialized, and previous information
on them is overwritten.
.TP
.B x
The named files are extracted from the diskette.
If the named
file matches a directory whose contents had been written onto
the diskette, this directory is (recursively) extracted.
The owner,
modification time, and mode are restored (if possible).
If no file
argument is given, the entire content of the medium is
extracted.
Note that if multiple entries specifying the same file
are on the diskette, the last one (most recently saved) overwrites
all earlier.
.TP
.B t
The names of the specified files are listed each time they occur
on the diskette.
If no file argument is given, all of the names
on the diskette are listed.
.TP
.B u
The named files are added to the diskette if either they are not
already there or have been modified since last put on the
diskette.
This option can not be used to update previous
.BR tar 's
which have spread across multiple media; only the currently
loaded diskette is looked at when deciding what files need
updating.
(However, the files written out may spread over
several volumes as normal).
.PP
The following characters may be used in addition to the letter which
selects the function desired.
.TP 1i
.B v
Normally
.B tar
does its work silently.
The
.B v
(verbose) option
causes it to type the name of each file it treats preceded by
the function letter.
With the
.B t
function,
.B v
gives more information
about the diskette entries than just the name.
When
used with the
.BR c ,
.BR r ,
or
.B t
options, it also causes
.B tar
to list the
total number of blocks used on the volume, giving the user a
rough idea of what room is left on the volume for more files.
.TP
.B s
The next argument gives the size of the output device in
blocks.
.TP
.B d
Only save or restore files modified within the last number of
days specified by the next argument.
.B tar
will calculate and
print the effective date.
When using this flag to selectively
restore files from tape or disk, the date compared against for
each file is the last-modified date of the file when it was
.BR tar 'ed
out, not the date that
.B tar
occurred.
.TP
.B w
causes
.B tar
to print the action to be taken followed by file
name, then wait for user confirmation.
If a word beginning
with `y' is given, the action is performed.
Any other input
means don't do it.
.TP
.B f
causes
.B tar
to use the next argument as the name of the output
device instead of the default name.
If the name of the file is
`\-', tar writes to standard output or reads from standard
input, whichever is appropriate.
Thus,
.B tar
can be used as the
head or tail of a filter chain.
.B tar
can also be used to move
hierarchies with the command
.br
.ti +.5i
cd fromdir; tar cf \- . | (cd todir; tar xf \-)
.TP
.B b
causes
.B tar
to use the next argument as the blocking factor for
diskette (or tape) records.
The default is 1, the maximum is
20.
The block size is determined automatically when reading
disks (key letters `x' and `t').
On magtape, if block size is
other than 1, a
.B f
key (above) should be used to specify the
raw version of the device.
On devices other than magtape,
block size is not normally important.
.TP
.B l
tells
.B tar
to complain if it cannot resolve all of the links to the
files dumped.
If this is not specified, no error messages are
printed.
.TP
.B m
tells
.B tar
to not restore the modification times.
The mod time
will be the time of extraction.
.TP
.B 0,...,7
A digit 0 through 7 as a modifier selects the unit number to
use on the floppy or tape drive (see notes on
.B f
key to determine
your default).
Thus on systems whose default device is
floppy drive 0
.RB ( /dev/f0 ),
files may be saved on floppy drive 1
by typing
.B "tar c1..."
\&.
This is equivalent to typing
.B "tar cf /dev/f1..."
\&.
.PP
The number of blocks used by
.B tar
when writing a volume can be calculated
by adding the number of blocks in all files, plus one additional
header block per file, plus 2 blocks for the volume trailer.
.PP
.B tar
will not understand `*', `?' or other wildcard notation when extracting
files.
.PP
.B tar
will not work with mountable diskettes; using
.B tar
to write on a
mountable diskette will destroy it, and attempting to extract files from a
mountable disk using
.B tar
will cause error messages.
Similarly, attempts
to mount a
.B tar
format diskette are ill-fated.
.SH FILES
.nf
/dev/f?
/tmp/tar*
awk, sort, pwd, mkdir(1)
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Complains about bad key characters and diskette read/write errors.
.br
Complains if enough memory is not available to hold the link tables.
.SH EXAMPLES
To store the directories
.I nile
and
.I cairo
on the default device (floppy or
tape):
.IP
tar c nile cairo
.PP
To add the file
.I pyramids
in the directory
.IR cairo :
.IP
tar r cairo/pyramids
.PP
To add another file
.I sphinx
to the end of the last file on floppy unit 1:
.IP
tar rvf /dev/f1 sphinx
.PP
.BR tar ,
when used with the
.B v
flag, will also tell you how many blocks have
been used on the medium.
.PP
To create a new diskette in drive 1 holding only those files in
.I cairo
modified in the last seven days:
.IP
tar cdf 7 /dev/f1 cairo
.PP
To view the files on the default
.B tar
device, simply type:
.IP
tar tv
.PP
To extract all files:
.IP
tar xv
.PP
To extract a specific file in a directory, for example, file
.I pyramids
in the
directory
.IR cairo :
.IP
tar xv cairo/pyramids
.PP
.B tar
always restores files using the same pathnames given when the files
were saved.
This means that full pathnames (that is, names beginning
with a `/') should be avoided.
For example, suppose that user Dennis
has a home directory
.IP
/usr/dennis
.PP
and a directory beneath that:
.IP
/usr/dennis/letters
.PP
If directory
.I letters
is saved using the command
.IP
tar c /usr/dennis/letters
.PP
a subsequent extraction, with
.IP
tar x
.PP
will always bring back the old files to
.IR /usr/dennis/letters ,
overwriting
any files of the same name in that directory.
For this reason, it is desirable
to save files according to a ``relative'' name, for example from
Dennis' home directory
.RI ( /usr/dennis ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta 2.2i
cd	(change directory to /usr/dennis)
tar c letters
.fi
.RE
.PP
Now the command
.IP
tar x
.PP
will extract the saved files into whatever directory Dennis is in when he
issues the command.
So to restore the saved files to a temporary directory,
the following commands could be used:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd
mkdir temp
cd temp
tar x
.fi
.RE
.PP
Now the extracted files are placed in
.IP
/usr/dennis/temp/letters
.PP
while the directory
.IP
/usr/dennis/letters
.PP
is untouched.
Of course, if Dennis really wishes to extract his files into
.IR /usr/dennis/letters ,
and overwrite any copies of the same files on disk,
he can always use the commands
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd
tar x
.fi
.RE
.PP
and this will happen.
.SH NOTES
Unlike some other versions of
.BR tar ,
VENIX
.B tar
saves directory and device
node entries as well as ordinary files.
This feature does not affect compatibility
with other versions of
.BR tar ,
except that device nodes saved by
VENIX
.B tar
may be turned into zero-length ordinary files when extracted
by other versions of
.BR tar ,
and directory mode and owner information may
be lost.
Extraneous error messages may also be produced.
.SH BUGS
There is no way to ask for the
.IR n -th
occurrence of a file.
.PP
The
.B u
option can be slow.
.PP
The
.B b
option should not be used with archives that are going to be
updated.
If the archive is on a disk file the
.B b
option should not be used
at all, as updating an archive stored in this manner can destroy it.
.PP
The current limit on file name length is 100 characters.
.PP
The modification times of non-empty directories extracted by
.B tar
will
always be the time of extraction, not the time saved, since the
modification time becomes updated when the first file is created inside the
directory.
.sp
